rting  in 1934 the federal government  gave white people better access to  affordable mortgages  while designating black neighborhoods in  cities across america  as zones where banks should not offer  mortgages this was known as redlining  which encouraged white people  to move to homogenously white suburbs  and it trapped black people in decaying  neighborhoods  also in the 1930s the public works  administration  built tens of thousands of public  housing complexes all over the country  all of which had a designation for which  racial group was allowed to live there  and almost all of them were either all  white or all black and much of the  racial segregation  that it caused is still present today  there are plenty of other instances of  government mandating  racial housing segregation deliberate  codified  government racism is what gave us almost  all of the housing segregation we see  today and now  white people and black people are more  segregated from one another  then white people are segregated from  asian or hispanic people as  examples and more so than poor and  non-poor people  are segregated from one another and  black white  segregation puts a hugely unfair burden  on black people it has much worse  effects for a black family living in a  black neighborhood  who have the same income and education  as a white family living in a white  neighborhood  the average middle-class black family  lives in a neighborhood with a higher  poverty rate  than the average low-income white person  segregation also prevents home values  from appreciating  in black neighborhoods because of white  folks reluctance to buy a home in a  black neighborhood  even when all other attributes of homes  and neighborhoods are equal  which by the way is an example of  systemic racism happening  actively in the year 2020 and it's a  type of systemic racism with a huge  impact on black wealth  since someone's home is often their  largest financial asset  so the crucial thing here is that higher  poverty rates in the neighborhood  means lower quality resources for  everyone who lives there  segregation is the biggest barrier  between  black people and higher income wealth  better education health care employment  opportunities and so on  one might even be able to say that today  the government's refusal to integrate  housing more effectively  to fix the problems that government  created originally  could itself be another example of  ongoing systemic racism in 2020  the government not doing something is  just as much a policy decision as the  government  doing something sure someone like ben  shapiro  can sit back and say yeah okay whatever  happened in the past with racism and  segregation was bad but  now that the government has stopped  actively mandating segregation we can  just wipe the slate clean we can just  blame  individual black people for being poor  but that would be a complete  misunderstanding of the forces that  created these problems and the forces  that would be necessary to fix them  government has a ton of power obviously  and it used this power to segregate for  a long time  that same amount of power would be  required to undo it  this is where we get to the distinction  between desegregation  and integration desegregation is when a  government stops  separating people integration is the  process of unseparating those same  people which has not happened  so we're currently stuck in a state of  segregation essentially in a way the  civil rights act did not accomplish as  much as conservatives seem to think it  did  it didn't hit the reset button on racial  inequality racial groups in the united  states are not  on a level playing field so what can  government do to counteract segregation  as a way to give black people more  economic opportunity well  to start with we could reinstitute the  obama-era policy  of requiring localities that receive  federal money for housing  to identify and try to remedy housing  practices that promote racial bias  the policy that was suspended by the  trump administration which donald trump  completely blew out of proportion in  terms of what it did and  uh donald trump has made clear that he  has no interest in dealing with that  issue and in fact  in his personal past has a history of  racial housing discrimination  commenting on his elimination of this  anti-racist policy trump actually  tweeted  i am happy to inform all of the people  living in their suburban lifestyle dream  that you will no longer be bothered or  financially hurt  by having low income housing built in  your neighborhood enjoy  that's another example of institutional  racism in 2020 if i've ever seen one  another step we could take to counter  segregation  by reducing housing discrimination is to  increase  the use of fair housing testers who are  individuals who  pose as potential buyers and renters to  gather information  on possible instances of illegal  racial housing discrimination because  this discrimination tends to push black  people  out of white areas tests like these have  been funded by the department of housing  and urban development  and they've uncovered a staggering  amount of racial discrimination for  example they did a test in chicago  in 2018 and identified racial  discrimination  27 of the time there's  some more current systemic racism as an  example for you nevertheless  hud does not fund these tests anywhere  near the level  that matches how pervasive and  destructive we've observed this problem  to be  we have to be better about enforcing  anti-housing discrimination laws  that are already on the books government  could also be bolstering  inclusionary zoning policies which  require home builders to make sure that  portions of new housing units are  affordable to low and moderate income  people  to eliminate the financial barriers that  often end up keeping poor black families  from finding an affordable home in a  good neighborhood  we could also be using more federal  housing subsidies to  help lower-income black families move  into better neighborhoods the low-income  housing  tax credit could be used to develop  subsidized rental housing  in non-poor neighborhoods there is great  new research that it  illustrates that what we have been doing  with this  has had huge payoffs especially for the  kids  of low-income families there are many  other strategies  we could be using to um  disperse black families so that lower  income households  aren't so concentrated new policies that  can be implemented and  through better funding and enforcement  of initiatives that already exist  could be significantly beneficial but i  wanted to  explore just racial housing segregation  as an example  to illustrate some points about systemic  racism and how much can be done about it  it's a topic that is always relevant and  worthy of a deep dive  but especially right now with everything  going on with black lives matter the  discussions around race that are  happening  over the past few months while they've  had the country's attention  black folks in the u.s and allies  of all kinds have been trying to shed  light on a number of  really important problems in the country  things that this country has to start  dealing with it's way overdue  but i'm seeing some people actually  confused about what are even the  starting points for these conversations  the reality is racism can take many  different forms some people just have an  overly simplistic  conception of what racism is people  start talking about systemic racism  conservatives respond with  i have no idea what you're talking about  we have no more slavery jim crow is over  we ended that a long time ago so racial  injustice no longer exists  that perspective misunderstands  deliberately  or accidentally what systemic racism is  and it ignores  the fact that systemic racism exists and  is just another form of racism we also  have to refute  the common conservative notion that  black  hardships can only be productively  discussed  in the context of hardship or poverty in  general  instead of acknowledging additional  racial components that are often  involved  there are some issues that are more  accurately understood  when you take into account racial  injustices that may be at play  so that you can solve them with  specially catered solutions
 

